# ci-pool-attributed-gauges.service — one run of ci-pool-attributed-gauges.sh
# (see its header); fired about every minute by ci-pool-attributed-gauges.timer.
#
# Emitter for livespec-i4ahv4 (livespec repo, plan
# `ci-runner-pod-lifecycle-reliability`, epic livespec-ifwnqj) scope 1, 2, 4
# and 5: the pool's queue depth and runner population PER REPOSITORY, which
# the lifecycle sweep's fleet sums cannot express — and scope 3, the
# per-runner RUNNING-JOB detail, as wide events. Also for `livespec-ifwnqj.6`:
# the runner-pod START INTERVAL emitted as its PHASES rather than as one
# aggregate, as gauges beside the others and as a second wide-event kind in
# the same POST. Receiver: the
# CI-runner-host otel-collector (thewoolleyman/otel-collector
# config.ci-runner-host.yaml) on 127.0.0.1:4319, exporting to the `livespec`
# Honeycomb env. TWO POSTs to that one receiver per tick: the gauges to
# /v1/metrics, landing in the env's single Metrics 2.0 `metrics` dataset, and
# the running-job events to /v1/logs, landing in the `ci-runner-pool` dataset
# the exporter routes them to by service.name (it sets no dataset header).
# READER of both: the plan's Honeycomb board H2 (`livespec-mqy35a`). No
# trigger pairs with either — they are board inputs, not alarms.
#
# NOT A DynamicUser, unlike the sibling ci-runner-heartbeat.service: it runs
# as root with the k3s admin kubeconfig for its established all-namespace
# EphemeralRunner and pod listings and its cluster-scoped PersistentVolume
# listing. Those legacy reads need the admin identity. The ClusterQueue call is
# deliberately different: the script passes CI_POOL_KUEUE_KUBECONFIG only to
# that call, so queue usage is read through the scoped collector identity and
# never through the admin kubeconfig. It remains server-node-only because both
# kubeconfigs are rendered there and the server tick covers the whole pool.
#
# THE DEADLINE. The timer's interval is 60 s, so a run must finish inside
# one. The bounded work is FOUR kubectl list calls — ClusterQueues and
# EphemeralRunners at 15 s each, pods and PersistentVolumes at 5 s each —
# plus TWO 5 s curls, the metrics POST and the wide-events POST, so 50 s
# worst case, and TimeoutStartSec=55 keeps a wedged read from holding the
# cadence the way the lifecycle sweep's untimed child did on 2026-09-06
# (livespec-kgdlte). It was 50 against a 40 s worst case while this unit
# posted metrics only; the events POST added the fourth bounded step, so the
# deadline moved with it rather than being left to bite a healthy-but-slow
# run. A run killed at the deadline emits nothing, which is the correct
# fail-closed outcome.
#
# THE PER-PHASE START WORK ADDED NO DEADLINE, it REALLOCATED one
# (`livespec-ifwnqj.6`). The pod and PersistentVolume listings the start
# phases need cost 10 s of worst case, and the two loopback POSTs dropped
# from 10 s to 5 s each to pay for it: the worst case is 50 s before and
# after, and this line stays 55. Five seconds is not a weakened POST
# deadline — the collector is on 127.0.0.1, and a small JSON body it cannot
# accept in five seconds means it is wedged, which is precisely when this
# unit should stop waiting. The two new listings take the SHORTER kubectl
# deadline because the start signal samples by construction: abandoning one
# slow listing costs one sample, while overrunning this line costs the whole
# tick, gauges and events together.
#
# BOOT ORDERING is ORDERING ONLY (no Requires=), the shape
# ci-kueue-webhook-probe.service settled on: a boot-time activation waits for
# k3s and for the reconstruct converge to have applied the ClusterQueues and
# scale sets, so the first tick reads a converged cluster instead of failing
# and leaving a red unit that the next tick heals.
#
# Source of truth: livespec-dev-tooling ci-runner/observability/ — install via
# the ci_observability role of ../../ansible/ci-pool.yml, never hand-edit the
# live copy (the s2t recreatability rule).
[Unit]
Description=CI runner pool per-repository queue and runner gauges (OTLP gauges to local collector)
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
After=k3s.service converge-ci-stack.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# Runs as root (default): legacy pool reads need the admin kubeconfig.
Environment=KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml
# Only the ClusterQueue list receives this scoped, read-only identity.
Environment=CI_POOL_KUEUE_KUBECONFIG=/etc/otel-collector/kubeconfig
TimeoutStartSec=55
CapabilityBoundingSet=
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/ci-runner/ci-pool-attributed-gauges.sh
